{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310535,"nonce":"ANwGtbnuZvSHZefdoYN/bwHEEhs=","title":"tools/llama-diff/bench.sh prior to commit 9feb5b3 measured wall-time tok/s on a single short-prompt run that EOSed at ~7 tokens. The result ...","message":"**Rule**: when reporting a perf number, name the harness version (pre-9feb5b3 vs post-9feb5b3) AND the metric (wall-tok/s vs decode-only-tok/s). They don't transfer.\n\n\nThe old `tools/llama-diff/bench.sh`:\n1. Single run per side.\n2. Default prompt `\"What is the capital of France?\"` — the 1B Instruct model answers in ~7 tokens then EOSes.\n3. Reported `tok/s = N_REQUESTED / wall_time`, where wall_time includes model load + prompt encode + ~7 tokens of decode + tokenizer cleanup.\n4. Cache scrub (`rm -rf .rayzor`) before every run, so cold-start JIT bringup was paid every time.\n\nEffect: load_time dominated. Decode_time was ~3-5s of a ~10s wall. Optimisations that targeted decode were drowned in setup variance.\n\n\n- `RUNS=7` by default; reports median + range + stddev of decode tok/s\n- Default prompt is a long Paris history paragraph (override via `$1` or `PROMPT` env var); the 1B model generates the full N tokens before EOS at temp=0.01\n- Parses `[done] N tokens in Ts (X tok/s)` from the runtime's own decode-loop instrumentation; reports load_time and total_wall separately\n- Filters runs where `n=0` (JIT bringup hiccup) from the median, tallies them separately so the failure rate is visible\n- llama.cpp comparison path looped + medianed too\n\nEmpirical sanity (5 runs at N=64, Cranelift, with the nue/examples/llama-chat/rayzor.toml [tier] auto-upgrade-to-llvm config from a247060):\n\n  decode tok/s     median 1.738  range 1.718..1.759  stddev 0.029\n  decode time      median 36.81s\n  load time        median 0.51s\n  failed runs      3/5 (pre-existing JIT bringup crash flake — see below)\n\nRelative stddev 1.7% → any subsequent perf claim must move tok/s by ≥3% (~2 sigma) to be honest.\n\n\nRoughly 30-60% of runs fail with `n=0` (the runtime's `[done]` line never appears). Root cause is the `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry` path from a247060 — sometimes the LLVM bulk JIT compile after `module_init` SIGTRAPs during bringup. Reproduced by both the new bench harness and the matmul-profile workflow's instrumented runs. This is **not** caused by the bench rewrite; the new harness just makes the failure rate visible.\n\nThe bench harness handles it gracefully (filter + tally) but the underlying JIT crash should be investigated. Likely follow-up: add a retry-on-bringup-failure loop in run_file, or a flag that disables auto-upgrade-to-llvm so the bench can run without it for sanity comparison.\n\n\nCommits in `16e297f..8ed471d` cite tok/s numbers from the OLD harness. Do not compare them directly to numbers from the new harness — they measured different things. Approximate translation: old wall-tok/s ≈ new decode-tok/s × (decode_time / wall_time), but the wall_time fraction was non-constant across runs.\n\nThe \"noise\" verdicts for QKV fusion (8ed471d), bmm-threaded (5bda3e5), and Custom preset (a247060) were correct conclusions about wall-tok/s but tell us nothing about decode-tok/s. Re-bench any of those under the new harness if you need a defensible perf number for them.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_bench_measures_wrong_thing` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}